        body: 'Anaphylaxis is a **clinical diagnosis** (NIAID/FAAN). It is likely with acute onset of any ONE of:\n1. Skin/mucosa + respiratory OR hypotension.\n2. Two or more systems (skin, respiratory, GI, cardiovascular) after a likely allergen.\n3. Hypotension after a known allergen for that patient.\n\n**Current criteria \u2014 WAO 2020 simplified the 2006 NIAID/FAAN set from three to two, and the 2023 practice parameter update carries this forward.** Anaphylaxis is likely when EITHER: [28][29]\n1. **Acute onset** (minutes to hours) of **skin/mucosal involvement PLUS** at least one of \u2014 respiratory compromise, hypotension/end-organ dysfunction, **or severe GI symptoms** (WAO added severe GI symptoms, especially for insect-sting and drug reactions); **OR**\n2. **Acute onset of hypotension, bronchospasm, or laryngeal involvement after exposure to a known or highly probable allergen for that patient**, even with **no skin findings at all**.\n\n**\u26A0\uFE0F Up to 10-20% of anaphylaxis has no urticaria or rash.** The single most common reason epinephrine is delayed is waiting for hives that never come. A patient with acute bronchospasm or stridor after a known trigger meets criterion 2 with a completely normal skin exam. [28][29]\n\n**Other misses this pathway is prone to:** isolated GI symptoms with an implicated food; hypotension attributed to "vasovagal" after a sting or injection; a patient on beta blockers whose tachycardia is blunted; and biphasic reactions that recur hours after apparent resolution.',
