        title: 'Toxic / Metabolic - Treat',
        body: 'Open [CO Toxicity](#/tree/co-toxicity) or [Methemoglobinemia](#/tree/methemoglobinemia).\n\n- **CO:** 100% oxygen immediately; consider hyperbaric per criteria (syncope, ischemia, neuro deficit, pregnancy, high COHb).\n- **Methemoglobinemia:** methylene blue for symptomatic levels; remove the offending agent.\n- **Metabolic acidosis:** treat the cause (DKA, salicylate, toxic alcohol); a tiring compensatory tachypnea is an intubation trap \u2014 match minute ventilation if you must intubate.\n\n**Pitfall:** a normal pulse ox does not exclude CO poisoning.\n\n---\n\n**Carbon monoxide \u2014 the numbers behind "high COHb":** [18]\n- **High-flow 100% oxygen by tight non-rebreather immediately** (or via the ETT if intubated). This shortens the COHb half-life from roughly 4-5 hours on room air to about 60-90 minutes.\n- **Hyperbaric oxygen** is generally considered for **COHb \u226525%**, or **\u226520% in pregnancy**, or at **any COHb level** with loss of consciousness/syncope, neurologic deficit, myocardial ischemia (ECG changes or troponin rise), or persistent metabolic acidosis. Discuss with the hyperbaric service or poison center early \u2014 benefit is time-dependent.\n- **COHb level does not track severity.** A modest level in a symptomatic patient still warrants aggressive treatment; a high level in an asymptomatic chronic smoker may not.\n- **Suspect concomitant cyanide toxicity** in enclosed-space fire victims with a profound lactic acidosis out of proportion to their CO level \u2014 consider hydroxocobalamin.\n\n**Methemoglobinemia \u2014 the dose and its caveats:** [19]\n- **Methylene blue 1-2 mg/kg IV over 5 minutes** is the antidote, generally for **MetHb >20-30%** or for symptomatic patients at lower levels (ischemia, altered mental status, acidosis, dyspnea). Response is usually within 20-60 minutes; the dose may be repeated once.\n- **Remove the offending agent.** Common culprits: dapsone, benzocaine and other topical/local anesthetics, nitrites and nitrates, phenazopyridine, sulfonamides, aniline dyes.\n- **\u26A0\uFE0F G6PD deficiency:** methylene blue is largely ineffective (it needs NADPH) and can precipitate hemolysis. Use ascorbic acid, exchange transfusion, or hyperbaric oxygen instead.\n- **\u26A0\uFE0F Serotonin syndrome risk:** methylene blue is an MAO inhibitor. In patients on SSRIs/SNRIs/MAOIs, weigh the risk \u2014 but do not withhold it from a critically hypoxic patient.\n- **Dapsone-induced methemoglobinemia is prolonged and recurs** because of the long half-life and active metabolites; anticipate rebound and add cimetidine.\n\n**Compensated metabolic acidosis \u2014 the intubation trap made explicit:** a salicylate or DKA patient with a respiratory rate of 40 is not "failing," they are **compensating**. Sedating and paralyzing them collapses that compensation and the pH falls off a cliff at induction. If intubation is unavoidable, **match the pre-intubation minute ventilation** and treat the acidosis (bicarbonate for salicylate, insulin/fluids for DKA, dialysis for toxic alcohol) rather than the number. Screen with [HOp Killers](#/tree/hop-killers) first.\n\n**In salicylate toxicity specifically, intubation is associated with deterioration and death** \u2014 pursue alkalinization and urgent dialysis instead, and involve nephrology/toxicology before the airway.',
